          %h2#Idea Idea
          %p
            Beyond the speculative hypothesized role of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
            as a physical theory of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+strings"} fundamental strings
            that constitute the observed
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+particles"} fundamental particles
            in the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/standard+model+of+particle+physics"} standard model of particle physics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            as such, both on the conceptual structure of QFT as well as on concrete theories and their concrete properties. This entry lists such instances of string theory results having led to insights in non-stringy physics and in particular into experimentally confirmed physics, such as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/QCD"} QCD
            in the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/standard+model+of+particle+physics"} standard model of particle physics
          %blockquote
            %p Le plus court chemin entre deux vérités dans le domaine réel passe par le domaine complexe.
            %p
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jacques+Hadamard"} Jacques Hadamard
              (
              %a{:href => "http://homepage.math.uiowa.edu/~jorgen/hadamardquotesource.html"}> source
              )
          %p
            %br/
          %blockquote
            %p The construction of efficient computational methods for field theory scattering amplitudes has benefited substantially from string theory input.
            %p
              %a{:href => "#RoibanVolovich04"} Roiban &amp; Volovich 2004
          %h2#examples Examples
          %p
            The two basic theories that underlie observed fundamental physics – and which string theory unifies at least qualitatively and in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbation+theory"} perturbation theory
            – are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yang-Mills+theory"} Yang-Mills theory
            and
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravity"} Einstein gravity
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/general+relativity"} general relativity
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                %a{:href => "#ApplicationsToYangMillsTheory"} Applications to Yang-Mills theory
            %li
              %p
                %a{:href => "#ApplicationsToGravity"} Applications to Gravity
          %p Many of the insights are based on the gauge/gravity duality in string theory:
          %ol
            %li
              %a{:href => ""} General relation between Yang-Mills theory and gravity
          %h3#worldline_formalism Worldline formalism
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldline+formalism"} worldline formalism
            for expressing QFT
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scattering+amplitudes"} scattering amplitudes
            in an effective
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+invariance"} gauge invariant
            way (different from but equivalent to the
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+rules"} Feynman rules
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+scattering+amplitudes"} string scattering amplitudes
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldline+formalism"} worldline formalism
            for more.
          %p Example:
          %p
            The first calculation along these lines was actually done earlier in (
            %a{:href => "#MetsaevTseytlin88"}> Metsaev-Tseytlin 88
            ), where the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/1-loop"} 1-loop
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/beta+function"} beta function
            for pure
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yang-Mills+theory"} Yang-Mills theory
            was obtained as the point-particle limit of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/partition+function"} partition function
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bosonic+string"} bosonic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+string"} open string
            in a Yang-Mills
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/background+field"} background field
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Nepomechie83"} Nepomechie 83
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dimensional+regularization"} dimensional regularization
            then this
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/beta+function"} beta function
            coefficient of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yang-Mills+theory"} Yang-Mills theory
            vanishes in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dimension"} dimension
            26. This of course is the critical dimension of the
            = succeed "." do
